<Project>

  <!-- Sibling AsyncResponse.* packages are packed as EXACT-version dependencies: [1.2.3], not the
       ">= 1.2.3" NuGet writes for a ProjectReference by default.

       The packages are one product split along provider lines, not independently versioned
       libraries. Core grants InternalsVisibleTo to every provider package and Abstractions grants
       it to Core and the database channels, and the providers compile against those internals
       (AsyncResponseIngress, the envelope JSON, FlowStateJson, JsonSafety, the startup markers, …)
       — a surface no public-API baseline tracks and that changes freely between releases. With an open range NuGet happily
       resolves AsyncResponse.Core 0.2.0 next to AsyncResponse.Transports.Redis 0.1.0, and the
       application dies with MissingMethodException at the first call across the seam instead of
       failing at restore. With the exact range the same graph is NU1608 (a direct reference
       outside a dependency's range) or NU1107 (two providers pinning different Cores).

       Every ProjectReference under src/ is a sibling package, so every one is pinned — including
       Core -> Abstractions, which is NOT public-API-only for this purpose (see the IVT list in
       AsyncResponse.Abstractions.csproj).

       _GetProjectReferenceVersions is a private NuGet.Build.Tasks.Pack target: it fills
       _ProjectReferencesWithVersions, whose ProjectVersion metadata the pack task parses as a
       version RANGE. There is no public knob for this (NuGet/Home#5556), so the hook is guarded
       from the outside instead: scripts/verify-package-dependencies.sh reads the packed .nuspec
       files in CI and before every publish, and fails when a sibling dependency is not exact —
       which is what an SDK that renamed the target would produce. -->
  <Target Name="PinSiblingPackageVersions" AfterTargets="_GetProjectReferenceVersions">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_ProjectReferencesWithVersions Condition="'%(ProjectVersion)' != '' And !$([System.String]::Copy('%(ProjectVersion)').StartsWith('['))">
        <ProjectVersion>[%(ProjectVersion)]</ProjectVersion>
      </_ProjectReferencesWithVersions>
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
